---
import CaseStudyLayout from '../../components/CaseStudyLayout.astro';
import CaseStudyHero from '../../components/CaseStudyHero.astro';
import CaseStudySection from '../../components/case-study/CaseStudySection.astro';
import CaseStudySubsection from '../../components/case-study/CaseStudySubsection.astro';

const sections = [
	{ id: 'overview', label: 'Overview' },
	{ id: 'problem', label: 'Problem' },
	{ id: 'constraints', label: 'Constraints' },
	{ id: 'exploration', label: 'Exploration' },
	{ id: 'solution', label: 'Solution' },
	{ id: 'outcome', label: 'Outcome' },
	{ id: 'reflection', label: 'Reflection' },


];
---

<CaseStudyLayout title="Build IRL — Julia Heo" sections={sections}>
	<!-- TODO: add role, timeframe, and tightened description -->
	<CaseStudyHero
		logo="/images/build-irl/logo.png"
		logoAlt="Build IRL"
		headline="Designing an MVP from 0-to-1 for the builders and joiners of club communities"
		role="UX/Product Design Intern"
		timeframe="January - May 2025"
		tags={['Product/UX Design', 'Web/Mobile', 'Social']}
	/>


	<CaseStudySection id="overview" title="Overview">
		<CaseStudySubsection title="One product had to work for both sides of a club community">
			<p>
				Build IRL was a 0→1 platform for starting, running, and joining in-person clubs. Research revealed two different problems underneath that idea: builders were overwhelmed by the work of running communities, while joiners struggled to tell whether a club was actually for them. Over 10 weeks, my team and I helped shape the core builder and joiner flows for the MVP, from research and product framing through high-fidelity web and mobile design.
			</p>
		</CaseStudySubsection>
	</CaseStudySection>

	<CaseStudySection id="problem" title="Problem">
		<CaseStudySubsection title="Running a club was fragmented. Joining one was a leap of faith.">
			<p>From 10+ interviews with club builders and joiners, two problems kept surfacing on opposite sides of the experience.</p>
			<img src="/images/build-irl/userinterview.png" alt="User interviews" class="case-study-image" />

			<div class="observation">
				<span class="observation__label">01</span>
				<div class="observation__body">
					<p class="observation__title">Builders were doing too much across too many tools.</p>
					<p class="observation__text">Planning, RSVPs, promotion, communication, and logistics were often spread across several platforms. Running the club started to feel like managing software instead of building community.</p>
				</div>
			</div>

			<div class="observation">
				<span class="observation__label">02</span>
				<div class="observation__body">
					<p class="observation__title">Joiners didn't have enough context to commit.</p>
					<p class="observation__text">A club name, description, and event details could explain what was happening, but not what the community actually felt like. Potential joiners still had to guess whether they'd fit in.</p>
				</div>
			</div>

			<blockquote class="pull-quote3">💡 Builders needed less work. Joiners needed more confidence.</blockquote>
			<img src="/images/build-irl/affinitymap.png" alt="Affinity map" class="case-study-image" />
		</CaseStudySubsection>
		<br>

		<CaseStudySubsection title="The challenge wasn't adding features. It was deciding what the MVP actually needed to solve."> 
			<p>Build IRL could have gone in a lot of directions — events, messaging, discovery, club management, profiles, onboarding, creation tools.</p>
			<p>But trying to solve everything at once would just recreate the same complexity we were trying to reduce. So we narrowed the product around two questions:</p>
			<blockquote class="pull-quote"><b>01</b> Does this remove meaningful work for builders?</blockquote>
			<blockquote class="pull-quote"><b>02</b> Does this give joiners enough context to feel confident joining?</blockquote>
		</CaseStudySubsection>
	</CaseStudySection>

	<CaseStudySection id="constraints" title="Constraints">
		<CaseStudySubsection title="The product was still moving while we were designing it">
			<div class="constraint-grid">
				<div class="constraint-card">
					<p class="constraint-card__label">⏳ 10-week timeline</p>
					<p class="constraint-card__text">We had a limited window to research, define the core experience, and move key flows into high fidelity.</p>
				</div>
				<div class="constraint-card">
					<p class="constraint-card__label">📝 0→1 product, shifting scope</p>
					<p class="constraint-card__text">There wasn't an established product to optimize. Features, flows, and priorities were still being worked out as we designed.</p>
				</div>
				<div class="constraint-card">
					<p class="constraint-card__label">💻 📱 Web + mobile</p>
					<p class="constraint-card__text">We explored the experience across both platforms, then had to rethink parts of the work as the product moved toward a more mobile-first direction.</p>
				</div>
			</div>
		</CaseStudySubsection>
	</CaseStudySection>

	<CaseStudySection id="exploration" title="Exploration">
		<CaseStudySubsection title="Before hi-fi, we had to get the flows right">
			<p>We mapped the core builder and joiner journeys in mid-fi first, using them to work through hierarchy, flow, and scope before committing to high-fidelity screens.</p>
			<p>This stage was intentionally loose. As we reviewed the experience with stakeholders, screens moved, flows changed, and some ideas were reworked once we could see the product end to end.</p>
			<img src="/images/build-irl/mid-fi.png" alt="Mid-fi flows" class="case-study-image" />
			<blockquote class="pull-quote3">💡 Mid-fi gave us room to make those changes quickly without getting too attached to polished UI too early.</blockquote>
		</CaseStudySubsection>
	</CaseStudySection>

	<CaseStudySection id="solution" title="Solution">
		<CaseStudySubsection title="Help joiners understand the club before asking them to join.">
			<p>The joiner experience couldn't just answer what, where, and when. Research showed people were also trying to figure out whether the community actually felt like a fit.</p>
			<p>So, we brought more of the club's personality and context forward — things like interests, visuals, and community details — before committing.</p>
			<video src="/images/build-irl/video-1.mp4" autoplay loop muted playsinline class="mockup-video"></video>
			<blockquote class="pull-quote">💡 The goal wasn't to add more information. It was to surface the information that actually helped someone decide: Can I see myself here?</blockquote>
		</CaseStudySubsection>

		<CaseStudySubsection title="Don't make every builder start from the same place.">
			<p>Builders came into Build IRL with very different levels of momentum. Some already knew exactly what they wanted to create. Others had an idea but needed help shaping it. And existing communities might already have a visual identity somewhere else.</p>
			<p>So instead of forcing everyone through one setup path, we designed three ways where builders could: </p>

<blockquote class = "pull-quote2"> 01: Create from scratch </blockquote>
<blockquote class = "pull-quote"> For builders who already had a clear vision and wanted full control.</blockquote>
			<video src="/images/build-irl/video-2.mp4" autoplay loop muted playsinline class="mockup-video"></video> <br>


<blockquote class = "pull-quote2"> 02: Generate with AI </blockquote>
<blockquote class = "pull-quote"> For builders who had the idea, but needed help getting past the blank-page problem.</blockquote>
			<video src="/images/build-irl/video-3.mp4" autoplay loop muted playsinline class="mockup-video"></video> <br>


<blockquote class = "pull-quote2"> 03: Import from Instagram </blockquote>
<blockquote class = "pull-quote"> For existing communities that already had branding and content they shouldn’t have to rebuild. </blockquote>
			<video src="/images/build-irl/video-4.mp4" autoplay loop muted playsinline class="mockup-video"></video> <br>


			<blockquote class="pull-quote">💡 We adapted onboarding to different starting points instead of treating every builder like a blank slate. </blockquote>
		</CaseStudySubsection>
	</CaseStudySection>



<CaseStudySection id="outcome" title="Outcome">
		<CaseStudySubsection title="From early concept to a conference-ready prototype.">
			<p>Over 10 weeks, we defined and prototyped the core Build IRL experience across both sides of the community. The final direction was approved by the team and presented at a conference, where it received positive feedback.</p>	
			<p> Since I didn’t have access to long-term product metrics, the next things I’d want to understand is this: </p>
			<blockquote class="pull-quote3"><b>Builder completion</b>: Do people finish creating a club once they start?</blockquote>
			<blockquote class="pull-quote3"><b>Time to publish</b>: How quickly can a builder get a club live?</blockquote>
			<blockquote class="pull-quote3"><b>Join conversion</b>: Does detailed club context help people feel confident joining?</blockquote>
			<blockquote class="pull-quote3"><b>Drop-off</b>: Where do builders or joiners lose momentum?</blockquote>
		</CaseStudySubsection>
	</CaseStudySection>


	<CaseStudySection id="reflection" title="Reflection">
		<CaseStudySubsection title="I got more comfortable designing before everything was figured out!">
			<p>Build IRL changed a lot while we were working on it. Flows I thought were settled would shift after a stakeholder conversation or once we saw the whole experience together.</p>	
			<p> I left the project much more comfortable changing direction when the reasoning changes, even if it means letting go of something I already designed.</p>
			
		
		
		
		</CaseStudySubsection>
	</CaseStudySection>

</CaseStudyLayout>

<style>
	.pull-quote {
		margin: 12px 0;
		padding: 0;
		border: none;
		font-family: 'Gabarito', sans-serif;
		font-size: 16px;
		font-weight: 400;
		line-height: 1.3;
		text-align: center;
		color: #4A4A4A;
	}

	.pull-quote2 {
		margin: 12px 0;
		padding: 0;
		border: none;
		font-family: 'Gabarito', sans-serif;
		font-size: 16px;
		font-weight: 500;
		line-height: 1.3;
		text-align: center;
		color: #4A4A4A;
	}

	.pull-quote3 {
		margin: 24px 0;
		padding: 0;
		border: none;
		font-family: 'Gabarito', sans-serif;
		font-size: 16px;
		font-weight: 400;
		line-height: 1.3;
		text-align: center;
		color: #4A4A4A;
	}

	.mockup-video {
		display: block;
		max-height: 500px;
		width: auto;
		margin: 24px auto;
		border-radius: 12px;
	}

	@media (max-width: 768px) {
		.mockup-video {
			max-height: 280px;
			width: 100%;
			max-width: 100%;
		}
	}
</style>